Comment: 
```
// @@@ If the index is greater than or equal to 0 and less than or equal to the initial size of the vector, then for every valid index i from the insertion index to the old size of the vector, the element at position i+1 in the updated vector is either null and the old element at i was null, or it is equal to the old element at i.
```

We need to refer to old size, old element at i, and new element at i+1 after insertion. Use this.get(i) maybe? Since method add(int index, E element) inserts using insertElementAt(element, index). After insertion, size increases by 1. Need to use observer methods: get(int index) returns element at index. size() returns size. Also maybe elementAt? get is public.

So we need assert: if index >=0 && index <= \old(this.size()) then for all int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); ( (this.get(i+1) == null && \old(this.get(i)) == null) || this.get(i+1).equals(\old(this.get(i))) ). Need to handle null equality: use == for null, else equals. Could use (this.get(i+1) == null ? \old(this.get(i)) == null : this.get(i+1).equals(\old(this.get(i)))).

But spec style earlier used .equals for objects, not null. In examples they used this.query(i).equals(\old(this.query(i))) assuming non-null? Not handling null. For simplicity, we can use this.get(i+1).equals(\old(this.get(i))) . Might ignore null case. But comment explicitly mentions null case. Could express as (this.get(i+1) == null && \old(this.get(i)) == null) || this.get(i+1).equals(\old(this.get(i))). That's acceptable.

Thus final assert:

assert (index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size())) => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); ( (this.get(i+1) == null && \old(this.get(i)) == null) || this.get(i+1).equals(\old(this.get(i))) );

Make sure parentheses correct. Use => as implication.

Return final code block.

