analysisWe need to produce an assert statement for the comment.

We need to understand the method: add(int index, E element) calls insertElementAt(element, index). The comment: 

"@@@ If the index is greater than or equal to 0 and less than or equal to the initial size of the vector, then for every valid index i from the insertion index to the old size of the vector, the element at position i+1 in the updated vector is either null and the old element at i was null, or it is equal to the old element at i."

Interpretation: Precondition: index >=0 && index <= old size (size before insertion). For each i such that index <= i < oldSize (i from insertion index up to oldSize-1), after insertion, element at position i+1 (i+1 in new vector) satisfies: either both are null (new element null and old element null) OR new element equals old element (i.e., this.elementAt(i+1).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i)))? Actually equality semantics: "or it is equal to the old element at i". So condition: (this.elementAt(i+1) == null && \old(this.elementAt(i)) == null) || this.elementAt(i+1).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i))) . But need to use public observer methods. We have elementAt(int index) method (public synchronized E elementAt(int index)). That's an observer. So we can use this.elementAt(i+1). Also need old version: \old(this.elementAt(i)). So the assert should be something like:

assert (index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size())) => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); ( (this.elementAt(i+1) == null && \old(this.elementAt(i)) == null) || this.elementAt(i+1).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i))) );

But we need to ensure we refer to size before insertion: \old(this.size()). Since size changes after insertion (size increased by 1). So old size is \old(this.size()).

Also note that after insertion, size is oldSize+1. So i+1 is within new size.

Edge cases: if elementAt returns null, calling .equals would cause NPE. So we need to guard. Use (this.elementAt(i+1) == null ? \old(this.elementAt(i)) == null : this.elementAt(i+1).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i)))) . But the comment says "either null and old element null, or equal". So we can express as ( (this.elementAt(i+1) == null && \old(this.elementAt(i)) == null) || (this.elementAt(i+1) != null && this.elementAt(i+1).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i))) ) ). Simpler: (this.elementAt(i+1) == null ? \old(this.elementAt(i)) == null : this.elementAt(i+1).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i)))) . But typical spec uses equality operator for objects? In earlier examples they used .equals for objects. So we can use .equals.

Thus final assert:

assert (index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size())) => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); ( (this.elementAt(i+1) == null && \old(this.elementAt(i)) == null) || this.elementAt(i+1).equals(\old(this.elementAt(i))) );

Make sure parentheses correct.

Return type void, so no \result.

Thus produce code block.

